The greatest distance between the particles will be at the moment when velocity of both particles gets equal
\(\therefore\) Using \(v=u+a t\), for acceleratod particle
\(\begin{aligned}
& u=0+(F)(t) \\
& t=\frac{u}{(F)}
\end{aligned}\)
Distance travelled by accelerated in time \(t\),
\(\begin{aligned}
D_1 & =O+\frac{1}{2}(F)\left(\frac{u}{F}\right)^2 \\
D_1 & =\frac{u^2}{2 F}
\end{aligned}\)
Distance travelled by constantly moving particle,
\(\begin{aligned}
& D_2=(u)\left(\frac{u}{F}\right)+\frac{1}{2}(0)\left(\frac{u}{F}\right)^2 \\
& D_2=\frac{u^2}{F}
\end{aligned}\)
Greatest distance between two particles
\(\begin{gathered}
D=\frac{u^2}{f}-\frac{u^2}{2 f} \\
D=\frac{u^2}{2 f}
\end{gathered}\)

Using conservation of mechanical energy.
Decrease in kinetic energy = Increase in gravitational potential energy
$\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow & \frac{1}{2} m v^2=\Delta U \\
\Rightarrow & \frac{1}{2} m v^2=\frac{m g h}{1+\frac{h}{R}} \\
\Rightarrow & h=\frac{v^2}{2 g-\frac{v^2}{R}}
\end{array}
Substituting the values, we get
$\begin{aligned}
h & =\frac{\left(4.0 \times 10^3\right)^2}{2 \times 9.8-\frac{\left(4 \times 10^3\right)^2}{6.4 \times 10^6}} \\
& =9.35 \times 10^5 \mathrm{~m}=935 \mathrm{~km}
\end{aligned}
